Target mathematical pipeline

Analytic L¹-normalized CWT

\\(W_x(a,t)\\)

Phase-based instantaneous frequency

\\(\widehat f(a,t)=\frac{1}{2\pi}\operatorname{Im}\frac{\partial_tW_x}{W_x}\\)

Complex coefficient reassignment

\\(T\_{j,n}=\sum\_{k\mapsto j}w_kW\_{k,n}\\)

Full oscillatory inverse

\\(x\_{\rm osc}\approx\frac{2}{C\_\psi}\operatorname{Re}\sum_jT_j\\)

Component inverse

\\(x_k\approx\frac{2}{C\_\psi}\operatorname{Re}\sum\_{j\in B_k}T_j\\)

The formulas above assume the conventional analytic wavelet with real positive \\(C\_\psi\\), and bin-integrated SST coefficients. The implementation must also handle the corresponding complex synthesis correctly.

Important distinction: full reconstruction from a discretized, truncated wavelet transform need not achieve machine precision under this standard inverse. The acceptance requirement is mathematically correct normalization, controlled approximation error, and demonstrable convergence. Component reconstruction must be validated separately.

## 2. Theory the agent must implement

### 2.1 CWT definition

Adopt the \\(L^1\\)-normalized analytic CWT:

\\[ W_x(a,b)= \frac1a\int\_{-\infty}^{\infty} x(t)\psi^\*\left(\frac{t-b}{a}\right)\\,dt, \qquad a>0. \\]

With the Fourier convention

\\[ \widehat x(\omega)=\int x(t)e^{-i\omega t}\\,dt, \\]

this yields

\\[ W_x(a,b)=\frac{1}{2\pi} \int\widehat x(\omega)\widehat\psi^\*(a\omega)e^{i\omega b}\\,d\omega. \\]

For an analytic wavelet, the response is supported on positive frequencies.

Define the positive-frequency admissibility constant:

\\[ \boxed{ C\_\psi=\int_0^\infty \frac{\widehat\psi^\*(u)}{u}\\,du. } \\]

This is the constant associated with the direct scale-integral synthesis, and must not be confused with the squared-modulus admissibility constant used in another standard CWT reconstruction formula.

### 2.2 Continuous inversion

Integrating over scale:

\\[ S_x(b)=\int_0^\infty W_x(a,b)\frac{da}{a}. \\]

For a sufficiently regular analytic wavelet and signal, exchanging the integrations gives

\\[ S_x(b)= \frac{C\_\psi}{2\pi} \int_0^\infty\widehat x(\omega)e^{i\omega b}\\,d\omega. \\]

Thus, under the ordinary analytic-signal convention \\(x\_+=x+i\mathcal Hx\\),

\\[ \boxed{ x\_+(b)=\frac{2}{C\_\psi}S_x(b). } \\]

For a real signal,

\\[ \boxed{ x(b)=2\operatorname{Re}\left[\frac{S_x(b)}{C\_\psi}\right]. } \\]

If \\(C\_\psi\\) is real positive, this becomes the familiar \\(2\operatorname{Re}S_x/C\_\psi\\).

For an already analytic complex input \\(z=x\_+\\), the corresponding reconstruction is

\\[ \boxed{ z(b)=\frac{2}{C\_\psi}S_z(b). } \\]

The factor of two here follows from the definition of the analytic signal and the one-sided Fourier integral. It should not be confused with the relationship between the analytic signal and the positive-frequency projection \\(P\_+x\\), for which \\(P\_+x=S_x/C\_\psi\\).

These factors must be derived, unit-tested, and documented. Do not select them by fitting a tone amplitude.

### 2.3 Discrete scale integration

For logarithmic scales,

\\[ a_k=a\_{\min}2^{k/n_v}, \\]

the integration measure satisfies

\\[ d\log a=\frac{da}{a}. \\]

The discrete approximation is

\\[ S_x(t_n)\approx \sum_kw_kW_x(a_k,t_n), \qquad w_k\approx\frac{\log 2}{n_v}. \\]

Use the actual scale coordinates and a documented quadrature rule. The constant-spacing approximation is appropriate for uniformly log-spaced interior nodes, but endpoints, irregular grids, and alternative scale discretizations need explicit weights.

The implementation must also test whether the chosen finite scale range covers the physical frequencies of interest.

### 2.4 Synchrosqueezing and its inverse

Estimate the instantaneous frequency:

\\[ \widehat f\_{k,n}= \frac{\operatorname{Im} \left[ (\partial_tW\_{k,n})\overline{W\_{k,n}} \right]} {2\pi|W\_{k,n}|^2}. \\]

Reassign each accepted weighted contribution \\(w_kW\_{k,n}\\) into an output frequency bin.

Define the squeezed coefficients as bin-integrated complex values:

\\[ T\_{j,n}= \sum\_{k\mapsto j}w_kW\_{k,n}. \\]

Then, for complete deposition,

\\[ \sum_jT\_{j,n} = \sum_kw_kW\_{k,n}. \\]

Consequently, the real-signal inverse is

\\[ x\_{\rm osc}(t_n) \approx 2\operatorname{Re}\left[ \frac{1}{C\_\psi} \sum_jT\_{j,n} \right]. \\]

For a component occupying a region \\(B(t)\\),

\\[ x_B(t_n) \approx 2\operatorname{Re}\left[ \frac{1}{C\_\psi} \sum\_{j\in B(t_n)}T\_{j,n} \right]. \\]

The same synthesis formula must work for arbitrary time-frequency masks. No original waveform or CWT coefficient matrix may be required at this stage.

### 2.5 Numerical qualification

The continuous identity presupposes a valid analytic wavelet, complete integration over the relevant scales, and appropriate signal regularity.

A finite discrete CWT introduces:

- Scale quadrature error.
- Frequency truncation.
- Boundary and padding effects.
- Finite FFT resolution.
- Possible imperfect analytic wavelets.
- Floating-point error.

The agent must establish a reliable frequency region and measure convergence under refinement.

Do not mistake a near-perfect dual-frame inverse for proof that the scale-summation SST inverse is accurate.

Normalization clarification for implementation: for an already analytic complex input \\(z\\), all its Fourier content is at positive frequencies, so the correct formula under the definitions above is

\\[ \boxed{z(t)=\frac{S_z(t)}{C\_\psi}.} \\]

The factor of two is required when reconstructing a real signal from its positive-frequency projection, or when constructing the analytic signal of a real input. It must not be applied again to an input that is already analytic complex. This is an important dedicated test for the agent.

