## Acceptance criteria

The milestone passes only when:

1. The standard inverse is derived from the selected CWT convention.
2. The wavelet admissibility constant is independently verified.
3. The finite synthesis response is measured and its convergence documented.
4. The standard inverse does not require the original input, retained raw CWT, or an original-signal residual.
5. The reconstruction factor is correct for both real and analytic-complex inputs.
6. Complete SST coefficient sums agree with direct CWT scale sums.
7. Known isolated oscillatory components can be reconstructed from selected SST regions.
8. The individual-component errors are reported independently of full-signal errors.
9. The implementation is reproducibly compared against ssqueezepy 0.6.6.
10. Any retained alternative inverse is explicitly distinguished and documented.
11. All existing Milestone 03 tests pass, except where the previous expectations were demonstrably based on an incorrect inverse convention; revise such tests with a written mathematical justification.
12. No new Python runtime dependency is introduced.

## Final engineering rule

Do not optimize for machine-precision full-signal reconstruction at the expense of scientifically correct component extraction.

A standard inverse with controlled, convergent discretization error is preferable to a seemingly exact global inverse whose restricted-band behavior is undefined.

Stop this milestone only after both full oscillatory reconstruction and known-component reconstruction have been independently demonstrated.

## 4. Suggested numerical acceptance tests

I would additionally require the agent to generate a machine-readable validation report with the following metrics.

| Test                             | Expected result                                         | Suggested initial target                                   |
| -------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------- |
| Analytic tone frequency          | Exact frequency in reliable interior                    | Error consistent with spectral numerical accuracy          |
| Complex coefficient conservation | SST sum equals weighted CWT sum                         | Float64 relative error near \\(10^{-12}\\)                 |
| Standard full inverse            | Reconstruct well-resolved oscillations                  | Relative error below \\(10^{-3}\\), subject to convergence |
| Analytic complex reconstruction  | Correct amplitude and phase, no erroneous factor of two | Same convergence criterion as real signals                 |
| Two separated components         | Independently reconstruct each signal                   | Initially below 1% relative error in a well-separated test |
| Reconstruction bandwidth         | Error varies sensibly with mask width                   | Demonstrate a stable low-error interval                    |
| Increasing voices per octave     | Improved or convergent quadrature accuracy              | Convergence trend, not necessarily strict monotonicity     |
| Julia versus Python              | Comparable outputs and reconstruction error             | Explain differences under matched mathematical conventions |

These are starting performance and accuracy targets, not universal guarantees. In particular, the 1% component-error target should be tested on deliberately easy, well-separated signals first. No such guarantee should be asserted for crossing modes.

### One additional test I strongly recommend

Take a synthetic signal consisting of two separated components:

\\[ x(t)=x_1(t)+x_2(t). \\]

Reconstruct both independently using the exact known frequency curves, then compute

\\[ r(t)=x(t)-\widetilde x_1(t)-\widetilde x_2(t). \\]

Report separately:

\\[ \frac{\\|\widetilde x_1-x_1\\|}{\\|x_1\\|}, \qquad \frac{\\|\widetilde x_2-x_2\\|}{\\|x_2\\|}, \qquad \frac{\\|r\\|}{\\|x\\|}. \\]

Also compute the reconstruction of the unselected SST bins. That is the SST-domain residual, which should not be confused with the original-signal difference \\(r\\).

This verifies three different things: each mode is reconstructed correctly, the reconstructed modes explain the original signal, and any omitted synchrosqueezed coefficients are accounted for.

